    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Vinnie Pasquantino** (76.21) and **James Wood** (74.33) headline our offensive output. **Vinnie Pasquantino** benefits from a massive 14% boost via platoon advantage against Montero and a favorable park factor, making him our primary anchor in the utility spot. **James Wood** is set for a high-leverage outing leading off against Snelling; while the park factor is slightly suppressive, his elite 0.378 xwOBA and the 19% SP skill advantage propel him into \"Strong\" start territory. **Josh Naylor** (67.01) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (61.14) round out our top-tier options, with **Tyler Soderstrom** specifically benefiting from a +16% park boost that overrides the difficulty of facing Bradish.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Kyle Karros** (40.48) on the bench. Despite a decent platoon edge against the southpaw Luzardo, his lack of stolen base upside\u2014a critical priority for Ghost Ride the WHIP\u2014kept his efficiency score just below the threshold needed to unseat our starters. We are also monitoring **Corbin Carroll** (58.71) closely; while he remains a \"Strong\" start due to his top-of-the-order positioning, his efficiency is slightly capped by an elite-armed catcher behind the dish (1.87s pop time) and the formidable McLean on the mound. \n\nFinally, managers should proceed with caution regarding our middle-infield starters. **Brice Turang** (49.84) and **Cole Young** (44.40) are facing significant headwinds today. **Brice Turang** has to contend with an elite Fried, and his stolen base upside is hampered by a catcher who suppresses base thieves at a 54% rate. Meanwhile, **Cole Young** is pushed to the bottom of the order (-8%), which severely limits his R and RBI potential despite the favorable matchup against Burke. Both are playing at the bottom of our acceptable threshold; do not hesitate to pivot if late-breaking weather or lineup changes emerge.",
